# LinuxShot desktop entry. Two things in this file are identity, not cosmetics.
#
# 1. The file name. It must stay io.github.minnyat.linuxshot.desktop in every
#    channel (AppImage, .deb, .rpm, Flatpak, tarball, manual install), matching
#    singleInstanceID in main.go. gnome-shell's window tracker prefers a
#    StartupWMClass match, but it also falls back to matching a desktop file
#    named <wmclass>.desktop - so a channel that ships this entry as
#    linuxshot.desktop appears to work while the portal records the
#    screen-capture permission against app id "linuxshot" instead. A user with
#    two channels installed then gets a second permission prompt and a second
#    entry in GNOME Settings' app permission list, and revoking one leaves the
#    other granted. One id, every channel.
#
# 2. StartupWMClass=linuxshot - exact, lowercase. Measured on GNOME 46.2: the
#    Wails window carries WM_CLASS = "linuxshot", "Linuxshot" (from
#    g_set_prgname("linuxshot"), which Wails sets from the Linux ProgramName
#    option in main.go) and no _GTK_APPLICATION_ID, and shell-window-tracker
#    matches StartupWMClass against the WM_CLASS instance name first and
#    case-sensitively. Without this line the shell cannot map our window to
#    this file, so xdg-desktop-portal's focused-app check does not see us as
#    the focused app: a first, non-interactive Screenshot request is refused,
#    and any grant that is recorded belongs to whichever app the shell does
#    consider focused (an earlier test run left one on the terminal).
[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Version=1.0
Name=LinuxShot
GenericName=Screenshot Tool
Comment=Capture your screen, annotate it, and share the result
Exec=linuxshot
Icon=io.github.minnyat.linuxshot
Terminal=false
Categories=Graphics;2DGraphics;RasterGraphics;
Keywords=screenshot;screen;capture;snip;snapshot;annotate;region;print screen;
StartupNotify=true
StartupWMClass=linuxshot
